---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/utils";

const open = `from power_pptx import Presentation

prs = Presentation()                  # the bundled blank 4:3 template
prs = Presentation("deck.pptx")       # a path, a pathlib.Path or a binary stream
prs = Presentation("brand.potx")      # templates and slide shows open the same way

prs.save("deck.pptx")                 # an ordinary presentation
prs.save("brand.potx")                # a template
prs.save("lobby.ppsx")                # a slide show, which opens straight into the show`;

const errors = `import zipfile
from power_pptx import Presentation
from power_pptx.exc import InvalidPackageError, PackageNotFoundError

try:
    prs = Presentation("upload.pptx")
except InvalidPackageError as exc:      # a file, but not a deck this library can read
    print(exc)                          # file 'upload.pptx' is not a PowerPoint package
                                        # (not a zip archive)
except PackageNotFoundError:            # nothing at that path
    ...

# Older handlers keep working: InvalidPackageError is all three of these
issubclass(InvalidPackageError, (PackageNotFoundError, zipfile.BadZipFile, KeyError))`;

const size = `from power_pptx.util import Inches, MAX_SLIDE_SIZE, MIN_SLIDE_SIZE

prs.set_slide_size(Inches(13.333), Inches(7.5))     # 16:9, both sides in one call
MIN_SLIDE_SIZE.inches, MAX_SLIDE_SIZE.inches         # (1.0, 56.0)

prs.set_slide_size(Inches(80), Inches(11.25))
# ValueError: slide width must be between 1 and 56 inches (914400 to 51206400
# EMU), got 80 inches (73152000 EMU); pass fit=True to scale the size to fit,
# keeping its proportions`;

const wall = `# A 7680 x 1080 px LED wall, at 96 px to the inch, is 80" x 11.25"
size = prs.set_slide_size(Inches(7680 / 96), Inches(1080 / 96), fit=True)
size.width.inches, size.height.inches, size.scale    # (56.0, 7.875, 0.7)

# Lay content out in wall pixels, then scale it by the same factor
px = size.scale * Inches(1) / 96
slide = prs.slides.add_slide(prs.slide_layouts[6])
slide.shapes.add_textbox(1920 * px, 120 * px, 3840 * px, 400 * px)

# The same arithmetic without a presentation, for planning a layout
from power_pptx.util import fit_slide_size
width, height, scale = fit_slide_size(Inches(7680 / 96), Inches(1080 / 96))`;

const order = `prs = Presentation()
for title in ("Intro", "Regional template", "Appendix"):
    prs.slides.add_slide(prs.slide_layouts[5]).shapes.title.text = title

# One slide per region, built from a template slide
template = prs.slides[1]
for region in ["North", "South", "East"]:
    copy = prs.slides.duplicate(template, index=-1)   # index: where the copy ends up
    copy.shapes.title.text = region
prs.slides.remove(template)

[s.shapes.title.text for s in prs.slides]
# ['Intro', 'Appendix', 'North', 'South', 'East']

prs.slides.move(1, len(prs.slides) - 1)   # send the appendix to the end
prs.slides.reorder([0, 2, 1, 3, 4])       # or give the whole order at once`;

const hidden = `appendix = prs.slides[-1]
appendix.hidden = True       # stays in the deck, skipped in the slide show
appendix.hidden              # True
appendix.hidden = False      # shown again; the attribute is removed, not set to "1"

# Drop a slide from a generated show when its data is missing
for slide, rows in zip(prs.slides, tables):
    slide.hidden = not rows`;

const show = `from power_pptx.enum.presentation import MSO_TRANSITION

show = prs.slide_show
show                         # <SlideShowSettings loop=False show_type=SPEAKER
                             #   use_timings=True show_narration=True show_animation=True>

# A deck that runs by itself on a lobby screen
show.loop = True                       # "Loop continuously until 'Esc'"
show.show_type = "kiosk"               # "speaker" (default) | "window" | "kiosk"
prs.set_transition(MSO_TRANSITION.FADE, advance_after=5000)   # 5 s a slide
prs.save("lobby.pptx")

show.show_type                         # PP_SLIDE_SHOW_TYPE.KIOSK - always a member
show.use_timings = False               # "Advance slides: Manually"
show.loop = "false"                    # TypeError - True or False only`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Slides & Presentations"
  description="Opening and saving decks, templates and slide shows; slide size and its limits; duplicating, removing, moving and hiding slides; slide-show settings."
>
  <p class="lead">
    The presentation owns what every slide shares — the file it came from, the slide size, the
    order of the slides and how the show runs. This page covers that level. For the members of
    each class see <a href={withBase("/api/presentation")}>API: Presentation &amp; Slides</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-save">Opening and saving</h2>
  <Code code={open} />
  <p>
    Templates (<code>.potx</code>) and slide shows (<code>.ppsx</code>) open like any deck. What is
    written follows the name you save under, not what was opened: a path ending in
    <code>.potx</code> writes a template, <code>.ppsx</code> a slide show, and anything else —
    including a file-like object — an ordinary presentation. So opening <code>brand.potx</code>
    and saving <code>deck.pptx</code> gives you a deck PowerPoint opens as a presentation, not a
    template with the wrong extension.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saving to a path is all-or-nothing: the package is written to a temporary file beside the
    target and moved into place once it is complete, so a failure part-way through leaves any
    existing file exactly as it was. That includes a <code>lint_on_save = "raise"</code> gate,
    which runs before anything is written.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bad-files">Files that are not decks</h3>
  <p>
    A path with nothing behind it raises <code>PackageNotFoundError</code>. A file that is there
    but is not a package this library can read — plain text, a legacy <code>.ppt</code>, a
    password-protected file, an archive missing its required parts, a part that declares a DTD —
    raises <code>InvalidPackageError</code> with a message saying which, however the file was
    passed in. A valid package that is not a presentation, such as a Word document, raises
    <code>ValueError</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={errors} />

  <h2 id="slide-size">Slide size</h2>
  <p>
    The width and height belong to the presentation, not to each slide.
    <code>prs.slide_width</code> and <code>prs.slide_height</code> set them one at a time;
    <code>set_slide_size()</code> sets both, checking both before writing either. Neither moves or
    resizes what is already in the deck, layouts and masters included, so set the size before
    adding content.
  </p>
  <Code code={size} />
  <p>
    A slide is at least 1 inch and at most 56 inches in each direction — PowerPoint's own limit,
    and the range the file format allows. A size outside it raises a <code>ValueError</code> that
    states the limits in inches. Something specified in pixels, such as a video wall, easily
    exceeds it; what matters then is the slide's <em>shape</em>, and <code>fit=True</code> uses the
    largest size with the same aspect ratio:
  </p>
  <Code code={wall} />
  <p>
    The return value is a <code>SlideSize</code> named tuple, <code>(width, height, scale)</code>.
    <code>scale</code> is what the requested size was multiplied by — <code>1.0</code> when it
    already fit — so content laid out for the requested size lands in the right place when its
    coordinates, and its font sizes, are multiplied by it too. A size already inside the limits is
    never enlarged; one that is too small is scaled up until its shorter side is 1 inch; a shape
    more elongated than 56:1 cannot be a slide at all and raises.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slide-order">Duplicating, removing and reordering slides</h2>
  <Code code={order} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>duplicate(slide, index=None)</code> adds a copy right after the slide, or so that
      <code>prs.slides[index]</code> is the copy (negative values count from the end), and returns
      it. The copy is independent: its own shapes and text, its own notes, and its own copy of
      each chart and the workbook behind it. Pictures and media cannot be edited in place, so they
      are shared rather than stored twice. Links to other slides still lead there; a link from the
      slide to itself leads to the copy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>remove(slide)</code> deletes a slide the way PowerPoint's <em>Delete Slide</em> does.
      It also leaves its section, any custom show and the outline view, and every shape or text
      run elsewhere in the deck that jumped to it stops being a link (the text stays). Its notes,
      charts and the pictures and media no other slide uses are not saved any more, so the file
      has no orphan part or dangling reference. Iterate over <code>list(prs.slides)</code> when
      removing in a loop, and do not use a removed <code>Slide</code> object again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>move(old_index, new_index)</code> and <code>reorder(new_order)</code> change the order;
      <code>reorder</code> takes a full permutation of indices or of <code>Slide</code> objects.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A slide from another presentation raises <code>ValueError</code>; to copy between decks use
    <code>prs.import_slide()</code> (see <a href={withBase("/advanced/compose")}>Compose &amp;
    Templates</a>), of which <code>duplicate()</code> is the same-deck case. Do not remove a slide
    by deleting its entry from <code>prs.slides._sldIdLst</code>, the old workaround: that leaves
    the slide in the package, in its section and behind every link to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In a deck with <a href={`${withBase("/api/presentation")}#sections`}>sections</a>, the sections
    follow these operations the way they follow a drag in PowerPoint's slide sorter, and stay a
    set of contiguous runs. A copy joins the section of the slide before it (the original's,
    unless <code>index</code> puts it elsewhere). A moved slide stays in its section when it lands
    next to another slide of that section, and otherwise joins the section of the slide now before
    it — so a move can change which section a slide belongs to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hidden">Hidden slides</h2>
  <Code code={hidden} />
  <p>
    A hidden slide stays in the deck and can still be edited, but the slide show skips it —
    PowerPoint's <em>Hide Slide</em>. Only <code>True</code> and <code>False</code> are accepted;
    anything else raises <code>TypeError</code>. Hidden slides are still linted (the
    <code>power-pptx lint</code> report marks them), and the
    <a href={withBase("/advanced/render")}>thumbnail renderer</a> includes them, so
    <code>slide_indexes=[n]</code> always means <code>prs.slides[n]</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slide-show">Slide-show settings</h2>
  <p>
    <code>prs.slide_show</code> holds the settings of PowerPoint's <em>Set Up Slide Show</em>
    dialog: <code>loop</code>, <code>show_type</code>, <code>use_timings</code>,
    <code>show_narration</code> and <code>show_animation</code>.
  </p>
  <Code code={show} />
  <p>
    <code>loop</code> alone only changes what happens after the last slide; an unattended show also
    needs an advance time on every slide, which is part of each slide's transition (in
    milliseconds). Give the transition a kind as well — <code>MSO_TRANSITION.CUT</code> for no
    visible effect — because LibreOffice ignores the advance time of a slide whose transition has
    none. In a kiosk show a click does not advance the slide, and PowerPoint always loops it; set
    <code>loop</code> too so other viewers do the same.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>show_type</code> accepts <code>"speaker"</code>, <code>"window"</code>,
    <code>"kiosk"</code> or a <code>PP_SLIDE_SHOW_TYPE</code> member, and reads back as a member;
    the four on/off settings take <code>True</code> or <code>False</code> only. Reading never
    modifies the deck: a presentation with no settings reports PowerPoint's defaults
    (<code>loop</code> off, <code>SPEAKER</code>, the other three on). The settings live in
    <code>ppt/presProps.xml</code>; their element is created when a setting first changes and
    removed again once every setting is back at its default, and the rest of that part — pen
    colour, recent colours — is left alone.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
